   We now face the problem of finding the point G, which is the intersection of the line BF and the circle H.
One way is to use yet another variant of the partway computation: Normally, a partway computation has the
form hpi!hfactori!hqi, resulting in the point (1 − hfactori)hpi + hfactorihqi. Alternatively, instead of hfactori
you can also use a hdimensioni between the points. In this case, you get the point that is hdimensioni away
from hpi on the straight line to hqi.
   We know that the point G is on the way from B to F . The distance is given by the radius of the circle H.
