49.1.4    Annotations
An annotation is a little extra drawing that can be added to a symbol. For instance, when you add two
little parallel arrows pointing away from some electrical element, this usually means that the element is light
emitting.
     Instead of having one symbol for “diode” and another for “light emitting diode”, there is just one diode
symbol, but you can add the light emitting annotation to it. This is done by passing the annotation as
a parameter to the symbol as in the following example:
                              \usetikzlibrary {circuits.ee.IEC}
                              \tikz [circuit ee IEC]
                                \draw (0,0) to [diode={light emitting}] (3,0)
                                            to [resistor={adjustable}] (3,2);

