\pgfmathfloatparsenumber{hxi}
    Reads a number of arbitrary magnitude and precision and stores its result into \pgfmathresult as
    floating point number m · 10e with mantissa and exponent base 10.
    The algorithm and the storage format is purely text-based. The number is stored as a triple of flags, a
    positive mantissa and an exponent, such as

                 1Y2.0e0]    \pgfmathfloatparsenumber{2}
                             \pgfmathresult

    Please do not rely on the low-level representation here, use \pgfmathfloattomacro (and its variants)
    and \pgfmathfloatcreate if you want to work with these components.
    The flags encoded in \pgfmathresult are represented as a digit where ‘0’ stands for the number ±0·100 ,
    ‘1’ stands for a positive sign, ‘2’ means a negative sign, ‘3’ stands for ‘not a number’, ‘4’ means +∞
    and ‘5’ stands for −∞.
    The mantissa is a normalized real number m ∈ R, 1 ≤ m < 10. It always contains a period and at least
    one digit after the period. The exponent is an integer.
