57.2.2   Using L-Systems in TikZ
In TikZ, an L-system is created using a path operation. However, TikZ is more flexible regarding the
positioning of the L-system and also provides keys to create L-systems “on-line”.

\path … lindenmayer system [hkeysi] …;
    This will run an L-system according to the parameters specified in hkeysi (which can also contain normal
     keys such as draw or thin). The syntax is flexible regarding the L-system parameters and the following
    all do the same thing:

     \draw lindenmayer system [lindenmayer system={Hilbert curve, axiom=4, order=3}];

     \draw [lindenmayer system={Hilbert curve, axiom=4, order=3}] lindenmayer system;

     \tikzset{lindenmayer system={Hilbert curve, axiom=4, order=3}}
     \draw lindenmayer system;

\path … l-system [hkeysi] …;
    A more compact version of the lindenmayer system path command.

