103.3.1   Predefined Decorations
The three decorations moveto, lineto, and curveto are predefined and “always available”. They are mostly
useful in conjunction with meta-decorations. They are documented in Section 50 alongside the other deco-
rations.

103.4     Using Decorations
Once a decoration has been declared, it can be used.

\begin{pgfdecoration}{hdecoration listi}
  henvironment contentsi
\end{pgfdecoration}
    The henvironment contentsi should contain commands for creating an path. This path is the basis for
    the input paths for the decorations in the hdecoration listi. In detail, the following happens:
      1. The preexisting unused path is saved.
      2. The path commands specified in henvironment contentsi are executed and this resulting path is
         saved. The path is then divided into different input paths as follows: The format for each item in
         {hdecoration listi} is
             {hdecorationi}{hlengthi}{hbefore codei}{hafter codei}

                                                      1114
The hbefore codei and the hafter codei are optional. The input path is divided into input paths
     as follows: The first input path consists of the first lines of the path specified in the henvironment
     contentsi until the hlengthi of the first element of the hdecoration listi is reached. If this length is
     reached in the middle of a line, the line is broken up at this exact position. Then the second input
     path has the hlengthi of the second element in the hdecoration listi and consists of the lines making
     up the following hlengthi part of the path in the henvironment contentsi, and so on.
     If the lengths in the hdecoration listi do not add up to the total length of the path in the
     henvironment contentsi, either some decorations are dropped (if their lengths add up to more
     than the length of the henvironment contentsi) or the input path is not fully used (if their lengths
     add up to less).
  3. The preexisting path is reinstalled.
  4. The decoration automata move along the input paths, thus creating (and possibly using) the output
     paths. These output paths extend the current path (unless they are used).
Some important points should be noted regarding the use of this environment:
  • If henvironment contentsi does not begin with \pgfpathmoveto, the last known point on the
    preexisting path is assumed as the starting point.
  • All except the last of any sequence of consecutive move-to commands in henvironment contentsi
    are discarded.
  • Any move-to commands at the end of henvironment contentsi are ignored.
  • Any close-path commands on the input path are interpreted as straight lines. Internally, something
    a little more complicated is going on, however, a closed path on the input path has no effect on the
    output path, other than causing the automaton to travel in a straight line towards the location of
    the last move-to command on the input path.
  • Although tangent computations for the input path work with the last point on the preexisting path,
    no automatic move-to operations are issued for the output path. If an output path starts with a
    line-to or curve-to when the existing path is empty, an appropriate move-to command should be
    inserted before the decoration starts.
  • If a decoration uses its own path, the first time this happens the preexisting path is part of the
    path that is used at this point.
Before the automata start to “work on” their respective inputs paths, hbefore codei is executed. After
the decoration automaton has finished, hafter codei is executed.
                          \usetikzlibrary {decorations,decorations.pathmorphing}
                          \begin{tikzpicture}[decoration={segment length=5pt}]
                            \draw [help lines] grid (3,2);
                            \begin{pgfdecoration}{{curveto}{1cm},{zigzag}{2cm},{curveto}{1cm}}
                              \pgfpathmoveto{\pgfpointorigin}
                              \pgfpathcurveto
                                {\pgfpoint{0cm}{2cm}}{\pgfpoint{3cm}{2cm}}{\pgfpoint{3cm}{0cm}}
                            \end{pgfdecoration}
                          \pgfusepath{stroke}
                          \end{tikzpicture}

When the lengths are evaluated, the dimension \pgfdecoratedremainingdistance holds the remaining
distance on the entire decorated path, and \pgfdecoratedpathlength holds the total length of the path.
Thus, it is possible to specify lengths like \pgfdecoratedpathlength/3.
                          \usetikzlibrary {decorations,decorations.pathmorphing}
                          \begin{tikzpicture}[decoration={segment length=5pt}]
                            \draw [help lines] grid (3,2);
                            \begin{pgfdecoration}{
                                {curveto}{\pgfdecoratedpathlength/3},
                                {zigzag}{\pgfdecoratedpathlength/3},
                                {curveto}{\pgfdecoratedremainingdistance}
                              }
                              \pgfpathmoveto{\pgfpointorigin}
                              \pgfpathcurveto
                                {\pgfpoint{0cm}{2cm}}{\pgfpoint{3cm}{2cm}}{\pgfpoint{3cm}{0cm}}
                            \end{pgfdecoration}
                            \pgfusepath{stroke}
                          \end{tikzpicture}

                                                   1115
When hbefore codei is executed, the following macro is useful:
   \pgfpointdecoratedpathfirst
       Returns the point corresponding to the start of the current input path.
   When hafter codei is executed, the following macro can be used:
   \pgfpointdecoratedpathlast
       Returns the point corresponding to the end of the current input path.
   This means that if decorations do not use their own path, it is possible to do something with them and
   continue from the correct place.

                            \usetikzlibrary {decorations,decorations.pathmorphing}
                            \begin{tikzpicture}
                              \draw [help lines] grid (3,2);
